meta {
  name: 8 Set avatar
  type: http
  seq: 8
}
put {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/customers/{{customerEmail}}/avatar
  body: json
}
body:json {
  {
    "avatarId": "avatar_07"
  }
}
docs {
  Which of the 24 built-in marks this account is drawn with. `avatar_01`..`avatar_24` — the catalogue is
  `AgendaBuddy.Library/Avatars/AvatarCatalog.cs`, shared by both sides so the client cannot offer an id the
  server refuses.

  A dedicated route rather than a field on `PUT /api/v1/customers/{email}`, because that one replaces the
  whole stored document — sending only an avatar through it would wipe `phoneNumber`,
  `subscribedProviderCollection` and `appointmentIdentifierCollection`. This one is a targeted `$set` on
  `avatar_id` alone.

  Answers:
  - `200` with the updated customer on success.
  - `400` for an id the catalogue does not know (try `avatar_99`). ⚠️ **Refused, never stored** — an unknown
    id throws nowhere, because `AvatarCatalog.Resolve` treats it as absent and falls back to a mark derived
    from the email address, so storing it would leave the account showing its old avatar behind a `200`.
  - `403` for another account's email. Ownership-guarded.
  - `404` when no customer has that email.

  There is deliberately **no photo upload** yet (`agenda-buddy-5qr`): this product has no image storage, so
  that needs a storage decision first. This is the write path a photo would hang off.

  Verify with `2 Get customer by email` — `avatarId` comes back on the entity, and the read's 5-minute cache
  is invalidated by this write.
}
